course: history
lesson: 76
title: When civilizations meet: conquest, colonies and freedom, seen from both sides
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Imagine a stranger walks into your house, plants a flag in the kitchen and announces he has «discovered» it while your family is eating dinner; that is why many teachers now put a question mark after «Columbus discovered America»: millions of people already lived there, and for them 1492 was an arrival, then an invasion.
explain: In Europa Universalis you colonise «empty» provinces with a few natives in them, and a handful of conquistadors can take an empire; real conquests needed local allies who hated their old rulers, and diseases that the newcomers did not even understand.
explain: Empires look different from each side: one grandad remembers railways, another remembers famines, and historians still argue how to weigh the good and the harm; both stories can be partly true at once.
explain: The story also has peoples who fought back and won, like Ethiopia at Adwa and the Haitians who freed themselves, countries that kept free by clever deals, like Siam, and non-European empires too, like Japan in Korea.
explain: A fair historian listens for both sides, uses words carefully, and remembers that the people on the map were people, not numbers.
fact: People reached the Americas at least 15,000 years ago, and millions lived there in 1492 in cities, farms and villages; Norse sailors had reached Newfoundland around 1000, and Columbus began lasting contact between Europe and the Americas.
fact: Hernán Cortés had a few hundred Spanish soldiers but tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, such as the Tlaxcalans, who hated Aztec rule; smallpox struck the Aztec capital in 1520, and the allies later found themselves under Spanish rule.
fact: By 1914 almost all of Africa was ruled by European countries; Ethiopia defeated an Italian army at Adwa in 1896 and stayed independent, as did Liberia, though Italy occupied Ethiopia from 1936 to 1941.
fact: Britain ruled most of India from 1858 to 1947, after the East India Company; it built railways and courts, but took wealth, and several terrible famines happened, and historians still argue how to weigh the good and the harm.
fact: India won independence in 1947 after a long movement, with Gandhi leading non-violent protest, and was split into India and Pakistan, which forced millions of people to leave their homes.
fact: Between 1945 and 1975 dozens of colonies in Africa and Asia became independent; Ghana in 1957 is often called the first in sub-Saharan Africa to free itself from colonial rule, though Sudan, independent in 1956, is sometimes counted first.
fact: Japan avoided European rule and built its own empire, ruling Taiwan from 1895 and Korea from 1910 until 1945; in some years Koreans were pushed to use Japanese names and language.
fact: Siam, now Thailand, stayed independent between British and French colonies through skilled diplomacy, clever talks and deals with both, but gave up some lands to keep the rest.
fact: Enslaved people in Haiti rose up in 1791 and won independence in 1804, making the first Black republic; in 1825 France forced Haiti to pay a huge sum before France would accept it as a free country, and the debt took Haiti over a century to pay off.
fact: Cree syllabics were developed in the 1840s by the missionary James Evans with Cree speakers, and some Cree tell that the script came from a Cree man; missionaries brought schools and writing but often banned local customs and languages.
fact: After the conquest, Nahua writers working with the Spanish friar Bernardino de Sahagún recorded Aztec memories of the conquest in the Florentine Codex.
fact: British soldiers took the Benin Bronzes from the Kingdom of Benin, in today's Nigeria, in 1897; in 2022 Germany agreed to return over a thousand of them and began handing some back.
exercise: Why do many teachers question the word «discovered» for Columbus? | Millions of people already lived in the Americas | Columbus never sailed anywhere; he stayed in Spain | The Americas had already been found by the Romans | Nobody at all lived there until about 1900
exercise: Who reached Newfoundland around 1000? | Norse sailors | Spanish soldiers | Roman legions from Britain | Chinese astronauts
exercise: Who helped Cortés defeat the Aztecs? | Tens of thousands of Indigenous allies, like the Tlaxcalans | Thousands of French knights sent over by the King of France | The Roman army, which had stayed in Mexico | Nobody; he won alone with his own men
exercise: What struck the Aztec capital in 1520? | Smallpox | A volcano | A snowstorm | A tsunami
exercise: Which African country beat Italy at Adwa in 1896? | Ethiopia | Egypt, on the Nile | Morocco, in the north | South Africa
exercise: What did British rule in India bring, according to different sides? | Railways and courts, but also lost wealth and terrible famines | Only happiness and riches for everyone, with nothing bad at all | Only railways, and nothing else of any kind | Nothing at all, either good or bad
exercise: How did Gandhi lead protest against British rule? | Non-violently | By leading a navy | By building castles | By writing video games
exercise: What happened when India became independent in 1947? | It was split into India and Pakistan | It joined the British army as one big regiment | It became a part of China | Nothing changed at all for anyone
exercise: Which country is often called the first in sub-Saharan Africa to win independence from colonial rule? | Ghana, in 1957 | Egypt, in 1066 | Ethiopia, in 1914 | Nigeria, in 1800
exercise: Which country ruled Korea from 1910 to 1945? | Japan | Britain, from India | France, from Vietnam | Russia, from Siberia
exercise: How did Siam stay independent? | By skilled diplomacy, and by giving up some lands | By having the biggest and best army on Earth at that time | Because no one in Europe knew where it was | By joining the British Empire as a friend
exercise: What did enslaved people in Haiti achieve in 1804? | Independence, making the first Black republic | A colony of their own somewhere in France | The end of all slavery everywhere in the world | A new king sent over from Spain
exercise: Why did Haiti stay poor for long after independence? | France made it pay a huge sum to be accepted as free | Haiti had no people left at all after the war was over | Haiti refused to trade with anyone in the world | Haiti gave all its money to Spain
exercise: Who developed Cree syllabics in the 1840s? | A missionary working with Cree speakers | A French king in Paris | Roman monks in Italy | A Japanese scholar who visited Canada
exercise: Why is the Florentine Codex special? | It records the conquest as Aztec people remembered it | It was written by Cortés alone to boast to his king in Spain | It is a long list of Spanish recipes from Florence | It is the oldest book in Europe
exercise: What are the Benin Bronzes? | Artworks taken from the Kingdom of Benin by British soldiers in 1897 | Medals won by Nigeria at the Olympic Games in the 1990s | Coins made in Germany and used in West Africa | Statues made in Paris for a museum in London
test: Mateo's book says "Columbus discovered America". His mate: "Facts, he found it first, no cap." What's the fair correction? | People lived there for at least 15,000 years; Columbus began lasting contact with Europe | Your mate's right: Columbus was the first human being ever to set eyes on the Americas, in 1492 | The Americas were empty until 1492 | The Vikings never got anywhere near
test: Diego's mate: "A few hundred Spaniards clapped the whole Aztec Empire, they were OP." ("OP" means overpowered; "clapped" means beat.) What's the fuller story? | Tens of thousands of Indigenous allies and smallpox were a huge part | Spanish swords were magic, so they really were OP, no allies or illness needed | The Aztecs had no soldiers at all | It was all the plan of the Aztec king
test: In Europa Universalis, Ava sends a colonist to a province with "native population 2,000" and it becomes hers. What does the game leave out? | Those people had their own land, rulers and lives, and many resisted | Native people always invited the colonists in and gave them their land as a gift | Colonies were always on empty land | Native population numbers were exact
test: Arjun's grandad: "The British gave us railways!" His mate's grandad: "And famines!" Who's right? | Both are partly right; historians still argue how to weigh it | Only the railways grandad, since the famines were nothing to do with Britain | Only the famines grandad | Neither; Britain never ruled India
test: Hana, proud as punch: "We were never colonised, we beat Italy at Adwa, 1896!" What's the most exact version? | Ethiopia won at Adwa and stayed free, though Italy occupied it 1936–1941 | Ethiopia was never even attacked by Italy or by any other country | Ethiopia went on to conquer Italy in 1896 | Adwa is a city in Italy
test: Kwame's dad: "Ghana was first in sub-Saharan Africa, 1957, top of the class!" His uncle: "Ah, but what about Sudan?" Best answer? | Ghana is often called first, but Sudan, free in 1956, is sometimes counted first | The uncle is wrong: Sudan was never a colony of anyone, so it can't be counted at all | Ghana became free in 1800 | Both countries are still colonies
test: Yuki: "Japan was never a colony." Min-jun: "Yeah, but Japan had colonies, like Korea, ya know?" Who's right? | Both: Japan avoided European rule and ruled Korea and Taiwan | Only Yuki: Japan never ruled anyone at all outside its own islands | Only Min-jun; Japan was a British colony | Neither; Korea ruled Japan
test: Ploy from Thailand: "Siam never got colonised, nah, we was too clever." What should she add? | Siam also gave up some lands to keep the rest free | Siam was so clever it went on to conquer both Britain and France | Siam was a colony of China all along | Siam had no neighbours at all
test: Jean, whose family is from Haiti: "Our ancestors freed themselves." Her classmate: "So after 1804 it was all sweet?" What's true? | France forced Haiti to pay a huge sum, which hurt it for over a century | Yes, it was all sweet: nothing bad ever happened to Haiti after 1804, ever | Haiti became a French colony again in 1805 | Haiti gave money to France as a gift
test: Nathan's Cree kokum (grandma): "Missionaries helped make this writing, my boy, but they took things too." What does she mean? | Missionaries brought writing and schools but often banned local customs and languages | Missionaries only brought good things, and the Cree were glad of every single one of them | Missionaries only did harm | Missionaries invented the Cree language
test: What would happen if historians read only Spanish letters about the conquest of Mexico? | They would hear mostly one side; the Florentine Codex adds Aztec memories | They would know everything perfectly, because the Spanish wrote down every detail | They would hear only the Aztec side | Nothing, all sources say the same
test: Chidi's sister: "Germany gave back bronzes to Nigeria? Why's Germany got them, mate?" Best answer? | British soldiers took them from Benin in 1897 and they were sold to museums | Nigeria sent them as a birthday present | Germany made them in a factory | They were found on the Moon
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the term «discovery» in respect of the voyage of Columbus is subject to question. | The Americas were already inhabited by many millions of persons | Columbus did not undertake any voyage across the Atlantic | The Americas had previously been settled by the Roman Empire | The Americas were uninhabited until the twentieth century
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conquest of the Aztec Empire? | Cortés relied upon numerous Indigenous allies, and smallpox gravely afflicted the Aztec capital | Cortés prevailed solely by means of a few hundred Spanish soldiers | The Aztec Empire surrendered without any resistance | The conquest was accomplished by a French army
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the independence of Haiti. | It was won in eighteen hundred and four by formerly enslaved people, and France subsequently exacted a heavy payment | It was granted freely by France without any condition, together with a gift of money and ships | It was won in nineteen hundred and fifty-seven by peaceful negotiation | Haiti has never been independent
exam: Ascertain the means whereby Siam preserved its independence. | By skilful diplomacy and the cession of certain territories | By the conquest of the British Isles | By the possession of the largest army in the world | By its incorporation into the French Empire
